      "text": "To find the measure of angle \\(\\angle PQR\\), we need to understand the relationship between the angles in the given diagram. Let's break down the problem step-by-step:\n\n1. We are given that \\(RS\\) is a straight line, which means \\(\\angle RPS + \\angle PSP = 180^\\circ\\).\n2. Let \\(\\angle PQR = x\\). Since \\(QS\\) is a straight line, \\(\\angle QTS + \\angle TQS = 180^\\circ\\).\n3. We know that \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\). By the properties of angles and using the straight line, we can determine the angles in the context.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution\n\n1. Let \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\). Since \\(RSP\\) is a straight line, the sum of the angles on one side of the straight line is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n2. This implies that \\(\\angle RPS + \\angle QSP = 180^\\circ\\). Given \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\), we can find \\(\\angle RPS\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle RPS = 180^\\circ - 80^\\circ = 100^\\circ\n   \\]\n3. Since \\( \\angle PQR + \\angle RPS = 180^\\circ \\) (because they form a linear pair), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle PQR + 100^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle PQR = 180^\\circ - 100^\\circ = 80^\\circ\n   \\]\n4. To ensure accuracy, we can also consider the external angles property. Since \\(\\angle QTS + \\angle TQS = 180^\\circ\\), and knowing \\(\\angle QSP = 80^\\circ\\),\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\) is found to be \\( \\boxed{80} \\). Let's verify this using Python to confirm our calculations.\n```python\n# Calculating the measure of angle PQR based on the given information\nangle_QSP = 80  # Given angle\n\n# Since RS is a straight line, we know that angle RPS + angle QSP = 180 degrees\nangle_RPS = 180 - angle_QSP\n\n# Angle PQR and angle RPS form a linear pair, so PQR + RPS = 180 degrees\n# Thus, angle PQR = 180 degrees - angle RPS\nangle_PQR = 180 - angle_RPS\nprint(angle_PQR)\n```",
